[strategy/guidance/application/nodejs/GUID-TEC-NODE-002-bff-implementation.md#92-in-process-cache-for-high-frequency-ui-calls]

### 9.2 In-Process Cache for High-Frequency UI Calls

For reference data that is fetched frequently by the UI (plan codes, provider directories, formulary data) and changes infrequently, use a short-lived in-process TTL cache:

```typescript
// src/utils/ttl-cache.ts
interface CacheEntry<T> {
  value: T;
  expiresAt: number;
}

export class TtlCache<T> {
  private readonly store = new Map<string, CacheEntry<T>>();

  constructor(private readonly ttlMs: number) {}

  get(key: string): T | undefined {
    const entry = this.store.get(key);
    if (!entry) return undefined;
    if (Date.now() > entry.expiresAt) {
      this.store.delete(key);
      return undefined;
    }
    return entry.value;
  }

  set(key: string, value: T): void {
    this.store.set(key, { value, expiresAt: Date.now() + this.ttlMs });
  }

  invalidate(key: string): void {
    this.store.delete(key);
  }
}
```

```typescript
// src/services/plan.service.ts
import { TtlCache } from '../utils/ttl-cache';

const planCache = new TtlCache<PlanDetails[]>(5 * 60 * 1000);

export async function getActivePlans(correlationId: string): Promise<PlanDetails[]> {
  const cached = planCache.get('active-plans');
  if (cached) return cached;

  const plans = await planClient.getActive(correlationId);
  planCache.set('active-plans', plans);
  return plans;
}
```

**Cache key design rules:**
- Never use user identifiers as cache keys in a shared in-process cache. An in-process cache is shared across all requests; keying by user ID would cache user A's data and potentially serve it to user B if the key collides.
- Cache only non-personalised reference data in the shared in-process cache.
- Personalised data (member-specific claims, eligibility, care plans) must not be in-process cached — query downstream services directly.

